> On Aug 8, 2016, at 3:58 AM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Jaslene,
>
> The immediate answer is that the Rhat and ESS codes have trouble with constants, so
> you shouldn’t worry about those results.  Ultimately this is because there is a very thin
> line between a parameter not moving at all (constant) and a parameter moving very,
> very slowly (large autocorrelations).
>
> Note, however, that when you have missing data trying to simulate the missing values
> doesn’t actually do much.

In many models, it does absolutely nothing.

> The problem is that you can never recover the missing
> information, so simulating those values doesn’t improve the fit at all.

That depends on the problem and whether you have predictors
or not.  Semi-supervised learning's hugely helpful in natural
language processing!

>  It is much more
> preferable to marginalize the missing values out of the likelihood, for example
>
> p(d_obs | theta) = \int d(d_missing) p(d_obs, d_missing | theta),
>
> and then fit using the marginalized likelihood.

If they don't contribute to the posterior, they'll drop out as constants;
if they do contribute, this will be the most efficient way to implement
them.  But sometimes the integral/sums are too tricky.

- Bob

