From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Wed Aug 17 14:03:12 2016
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Wed, 17 Aug 2016 14:03:12 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Help with a simple, normal hierarchical model
Message-ID: <Om7cjP7_4KE.KKYxCQRoAAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <Om7cjP7_4KE.d6xnCO5lAAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <Om7cjP7_4KE.d6xnCO5lAAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/Om7cjP7_4KE/m/KKYxCQRoAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It's much more robust to parameterize in terms of Cholesky
factors.  We prefer the LKJ priors on correlation matrices
scaled by a vector of scales---there's a description of how to
do this in the manual chapter on regression.  And a paper
by Ben Goodrich showing why the LKJ priors make a lot more
sense than Wisharts (degrees of freedom parameter controls
prior concentration around unit matrix).

Also, you want to vectorize all those sampling statements for
efficiency.

To eliminate the divergences, you want to switch to a non-centered
parameterization (more efficient in both HMC and Gibbs).  There's
a description in the manual.

- Bob

> On Aug 17, 2016, at 3:24 PM, Natesh Pillai <natesh...@gmail.com> wrote:
