From u2567ef0395c70306 at groups.google.com  Tue Aug 23 01:47:51 2016
From: u2567ef0395c70306 at groups.google.com (pcoll...@berkeley.edu)
Date: Tue, 23 Aug 2016 01:47:51 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Marginalizing imputed sample size (yea...): Am I doing it right?
Message-ID: <Ry_m7ciVaUc.C_Q_6l4XAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <Ry_m7ciVaUc.moKHLFWPAAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <Ry_m7ciVaUc.moKHLFWPAAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/Ry_m7ciVaUc/m/C_Q_6l4XAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

So I've been puzzling about what to do with the dependence of the likelihood on both S and the value of μC0, which is also dependent on S. After some experimentation, it seems like I can incorporate both in the marginalization simply by putting an improper uniform prior (no prior) on μC0 and adding its normal probability density to the baseline negative binomial densities for each value of S. For example, for a single study with 30 observations in a single experiment:

transformed parameters{
vector[15] lps;
for (S in 1:15)
lps[S] = neg_binomial_2_lpmf( S | 2, 4)+normal_lpdf( μC0 | C0, sd_C0/sqrt(S) );
for (S in 1:15)
for (i in 1:30)
lps[S] = lps[S] + normal_lpdf( pts[i] | μC0-k * t[i], sd_Ci/sqrt(S) );

}

From ua7170d46507a5631 at groups.google.com  Tue Aug 23 04:47:53 2016
