MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

That's not unusual.  You see it in things like:

  y ~ normal(mu, f(mu));

when you have a scale that depends on the location.
that doesn't cause any problems with the generative interpretation
of the models.

Now if there are infinitely many discrete values (or even a large
finite number), then you won't be able to use Stan unless there's
an analytic form of the marginalization.

- Bob

> On Aug 23, 2016, at 7:00 PM, pcoll...@berkeley.edu wrote:
>
> Hi Bob,
>

>    In this situation, I have a discrete parameter that is used both in generating one of the parameters used to calculate the likelihood of the data, and in the likelihood for the data itself. Unless I'm mistaken, which is very plausible, the expression I wrote above is equivalent to evaluating the joint density for each potential value of the discrete parameter S, and is necessary to marginalize S out of both the likelihood of the data and the prior on parameter μC0, the 'true' mean starting concentration / intercept.
>
> Philip

