Rethinking whether my predictors are continuous or categorical.  Trap type is categorical, and for this analysis is best left categorical because in my ms I will focus on the effect of trap type on mosquito catch rates.  Date was the time between trap deployments: 24, 48, or 72 hrs (and it should matter as an area may be "over-trapped" and need time to recover its mosquito population).  Location was a rotation among forest trap deployment sites (4 sites) and we tried to choose sites that were equal in forest cover so I would hope that inclusion of "location" in the model will have little predictive value.  I also have temperature and humidity data, and could include this in the model.  Study was done in Iquitos, Peru (deep in the Amazon rain forest), so temp and humidity fluctuated very little during study.  Thoughts?

On Wed, Aug 24, 2016 at 4:08 AM, Richard McElreath <rmcel...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Great, sounds like all is well now.
>
> It's not necessarily a problem that parameters are correlated in the posterior. That's normal. It's just that chains with high correlations tend to mix less efficiently.
>
> That one divergent transition is from the final step taken to compute DIC. Scroll up and you'll perhaps see that.
>
> Both divergent transitions and the adapt_delta control parameter are addressed in later chapters, I think in Chapter 13 and Chapter 14, if not already in Chapter 12. It's a Stan setting, so if you search this list, you'll find lots of examples of its use.
>
> And yes, I'd standardize (not just center) all continuous predictors, just so the priors are easy to interpret and on the same conceptual scale (z-score scale).
